  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Dry and very warm through Wednesday.\n\nWeak high pressure and mid-level ridging will continue to dominate \nthe forecast heading into the midweek period. The high itself will \nslide offshore later today allowing the mid-level ridge to buckle \nheading into Wednesday. Meanwhile, a series of shortwave \ndisturbances will quickly slide from west to east across northern \nportions of the forecast region as a cold front drops south from the \nGreat Lakes and Ohio River Valley. This front will touch off showers \nand perhaps a few thunderstorms Wednesday afternoon and evening, \nmainly in areas along and north of I-66/US-50 and US-48. A Marginal \nRisk (Level 1 out of 5) for severe weather is in place for portions \nof western MD, eastern WV, and the VA Highlands Wednesday afternoon \nwhere better instability will reside. Coverage overall will be \nisolated to scattered with damaging winds as the primary concern. \nMost locations will remain dry through Wednesday afternoon given the \ntiming of the front and residual dry air to work with as the ridge \nslowly buckles. \n\nBefore the front arrives, expect near record warmth this afternoon \nand again Wednesday. Highs today will reach into the low to mid 80s \n(mid 70s mountains). Lows tonight will fall back into the upper 50s \nand mid 60s. Highs Wednesday will be much warmer in the mid to upper \n80s and potentially low 90s for areas south and east of DC. \n\nCooler conditions return briefly Thursday as more of a wedge like \npattern settles in. This is due to a large surface high pressure \nsystem anchored over southern Ontario Thursday wedging south along \nthe eastern face of the Appalachians. Expect cloudy conditions \nto prevail along with passing showers especially across the \nsouthern 2/3rds of the forecast region. Highs will return closer\nto normal with most locations in the mid to upper 60s. The CAD \nwill quickly erode Thursday night into Friday ahead of the next \nfront heading into the weekend. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...A strong front moves through the region late \nweek, bringing strong winds and cold conditions.\n\nA low pressure system is forecast to track from the Great Lakes \nregion into Canada Thursday night into Friday, pushing a cold front \nacross the forecast area on Friday. This frontal system brings \nincreased precipitation chances across the forecast area Thursday \nand Friday, with a few rumbles of thunder possible as the cold front \npushes through on Friday. Additionally, high temperatures in the 60s \nto low 80s cool to the 40s and 50s on Saturday as noticeably colder \nair moves in. Behind the front, temperatures plummet into the 30s \nfor most Saturday night with only the metro areas staying in the low \n40s. Temperatures will gradually warm on Sunday with highs in the \n50s to low 60s and overnight lows in the upper 30s to 40s.  \n\nBehind the frontal system, surface high pressure builds over the \ncentral CONUS beginning on Friday before shifting eastward. A tight \npressure gradient between the surface high and the departing low \npressure system leads to windy conditions across the forecast area \nFriday night into Saturday. Northwest winds gust 20 to 30 miles per \nhour , with higher elevations gusting up to 40 mph. Wind headlines \nmay be needed across the higher elevations but we will continue to \nmonitor. \n\nIt's also worth mentioning that with gusty northwest winds, upslope \nsnow showers are possible along the Alleghenies late Friday night \ninto Saturday morning. While snow showers are unlikely, a rain/snow \nmix cannot be ruled out.  Little to no accumulation is expected \nalthough the first flakes of the season are possible.",
